[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0,router1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: routing policy (Linux kernel/static routes overriding dynamic RIP) on router0 and router1.

Mechanism: Hosts in Location A (VLAN 10, 172.16.10.0/24) send traffic to router0 (GW 172.16.10.1). For destination 172.18.51.0/24 (Location C), router0 forwards per a kernel/static route to router1 (10.16.1.2). Router1, in turn, forwards per its own kernel/static route to router2 (10.20.1.2). Router2 “owns” 172.18.51.254 on eth1, but that interface is not connected to the Location C switch fabric, so packets are blackholed.

Localization and evidence:
- From pc4/pc5, pings to 172.18.51.x time out, while pings to 192.168.200.x succeed, proving router0 egress is up for other prefixes.
- router0 ip route/frr: K>* 172.18.51.0/24 via 10.16.1.2 (static), not RIP. RIP is otherwise healthy (R routes learned, e.g., 192.168.200.0/24 via 172.20.1.2).
- router1 frr: K>* 172.18.51.0/24 via 10.20.1.2 (static).
- router2 has 172.18.51.254/24 on eth1, but that port is isolated (cd_r2_fa1_0), and router2 cannot ping 172.18.51.15.
- router6 has 172.18.51.254/24 on a connected interface and can ping 172.18.51.15, showing Location C is actually reachable via router6, not router2.

Root cause: Stale static/kernel routes to 172.18.51.0/24 on router0 and router1 that steer traffic toward router2’s disconnected interface instead of toward router6.

Corrective fix: Remove static/kernel routes to 172.18.50/24 and 172.18.51/24 from router0 and router1 so RIP can select the correct path via router5/r3/router6; additionally, remove the stray 172.18.51.254 address from router2.eth1 to avoid future misdirection. This will restore reachability from Location A to Location C. === END ===